 ### 3. Review the task
 
 Per-task reviews are task-scoped gates. The broad review happens once, at the
-final whole-branch review. Never skip the task review, and never accept a
-report missing either verdict — spec compliance AND task quality are both
-required. Implementer self-review never replaces the task review; both are
-needed.
+final whole-branch review.
+
+**Review by risk, not by default.** Before dispatching a reviewer, read the
+task's risk off the plan — a `Risk:` or `Reviews:` line in the task text if it
+carries one, otherwise the nature of the work — and budget reviewer passes:
+
+| Task type                                                                              | Review rounds  |
+| -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------- |
+| Mechanical (renames, enum moves, CRUD mirroring an exemplar, contract regen)           | 0 (gates only) |
+| Normal feature (a service + endpoint + UI + tests)                                     | 1              |
+| High-stakes (Prisma schema, migrations/raw SQL, access resolver, auth, scoring, money) | 2              |
+
+**When the plan carries no risk signal, budget 1.** A round is one reviewer
+pass: the task review is the first, each scoped re-review in the fix loop is
+another. A mechanical task rests on the gates and the implementer's
+self-review — ledger that you skipped the task review and why, so the final
+whole-branch review knows the task was never reviewed.
 
+When you do run the task review, never accept a report missing either
+verdict — spec compliance AND task quality are both required. Implementer
+self-review never replaces the task review; both are needed.
+
